#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Run a build/gate command under three guards, always:
#   1. a slot mutex     -- at most DCAP_SLOTS (default 2) dcap runs at a time
#   2. a child scope    -- MemoryMax, so one runaway dies alone
#   3. a parent slice   -- MemoryMax, so N runaways still die alone
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------- slots ---
# This used to be ONE slot. It is two now, and the arithmetic is the point:
#
#   2 x 6G (DCAP_MEM per run)  <=  14G (dawn.slice)  <=  19G (the VM)
#
# Why one was right, and why it no longer is: the two global OOMs of 2026-10-09
# were not "two builds at once". They were ONE bare `bin/dawn check` that
# diverged in the checker: the java process reached 18.9 GB of OFF-heap memory
# while the heap stayed inside -Xmx2g (DAWN_JVM_OPTS="-Xss512m -Xmx2g
# -XX:+UseSerialGC"), so no JVM flag bounded it and nothing cgroup-shaped was
# around it. Serialising runs would not have helped; only a cap around the run
# does. A hook now forces every heavy command through dcap, so a divergent run
# is killed alone at DCAP_MEM and takes nothing else down. With that, the mutex
# no longer has to double as a memory guard, and a second slot is affordable:
# the slice (14G, of 19G RAM + 16G swap) still bounds the sum if both diverge.
# No JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS on purpose: the cgroup caps are the backstop.
#
# Swap: MemorySwapMax is 2G per scope and 4G for the slice. 0 would make a
# near-limit build die at the first page of pressure; unbounded lets a runaway
# thrash 16G of swap for minutes before anything dies (the 10-09 symptom). A
# little swap absorbs a spike, a lot only delays the kill.
#
# Each slot is its own lock file AND its own fixed unit name (dawn-build-0.scope,
# dawn-build-1.scope, ...), so both interlocks below hold per slot, unchanged.
# A run takes the first free slot, else polls until one frees up (DCAP_WAIT).
#
# Which slice: dawn.slice is a SYSTEM slice (/etc/systemd/system/dawn.slice),
# because the scopes are started with `sudo systemd-run` against the system
# manager. `systemctl --user show dawn.slice` asks the USER manager, which has
# never heard of it and reports MemoryMax=infinity: that is the wrong manager,
# not an uncapped slice. Always query without --user. The /etc file can lag the
# repo copy (scripts/dawn.slice), so dcap re-asserts the budget at runtime with
# `systemctl set-property --runtime` (same sudo -n it already needs for
# systemd-run); --runtime reverts at reboot and is reapplied on the next run.
#
# Why every invocation and not just "heavy" ones: bin/dawn silently does a
# two-stage self-rebuild whenever sources changed (bin/dawn:139,142), so
# `./bin/dawn --version` is a full compiler build. There is no such thing as a
# cheap bin/dawn call.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- layering ---
# Why a CHILD scope and not a cap on init.scope: everything -- the agent
# harness, your shell -- lives in /init.scope. Capping it makes memory pressure
# kill an arbitrary process there instead of the runaway. A child scope
# contains the kill. (Learned 2026-08-04: capping init.scope at 12G converted a
# VM-killing global OOM into a session-killing cgroup OOM. The receipt is still
# in /var/log/kern.log: `oom_memcg=/init.scope, task=python3, pid=2154`.)
#
# Why a SLICE on top of the scope: the scope caps one run. Nothing capped the
# sum. N concurrent runs cost N * DCAP_MEM, which can exceed the VM no matter
# how correct each individual cap is. dawn.slice caps the sum, so bypassing the
# mutex costs throughput instead of the machine. In the normal single-run case
# the scope cap binds first and the slice changes nothing.
#
# The same lesson one level up, in ~/.wslconfig: raising the VM ceiling to make
# a spike "survivable" moved the kill from Linux (contained, logged) to the
# Windows host (uncontained, silent). A cap in the wrong place converts a
# contained kill into an uncontained one -- in both directions.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------ mutexes ---
# Two independent interlocks, because the file lock has a failure mode that is
# silent on both sides:
#
#   flock locks an INODE, not a path. Delete /tmp/dawn-build.lock while a run
#   holds it and the next dcap creates a fresh file, locks the new inode, and
#   proceeds -- both runs succeed, neither prints anything. Reproduced
#   2026-08-05: two scopes overlapped for 4s, both exit 0.
#   Two things delete it: `systemd-tmpfiles-clean.timer` is active here with
#   `D /tmp 1777 root root 30d`, and flock never updates the file's mtime, so
#   an in-use lock ages out and is removed on a long-lived boot; and /tmp
#   accumulates enough litter to invite a manual `rm /tmp/dawn*`.
#
# So: (1) the lock lives under ~/.cache, which no tmpfiles rule touches, and
# (2) the scope carries a FIXED unit name. systemd refuses to start a second
# unit with a name already in use, which is a mutex with no path to delete and
# no inode to swap. It is checked by pid 1, not by a cooperating client, so it
# also holds against a run that overrode DCAP_LOCK.
#
# A fixed unit name has one failure mode that is worse than the bug it fixes,
# so it is not optional to handle: a scope whose payload dies by signal parks
# the unit in ActiveState=failed, and the name stays taken FOREVER -- every
# later dcap on the box blocks. Ctrl-C on a build is enough to trigger it.
# `--collect` makes systemd unload the unit even when it failed; measured
# 2026-08-05, without it the next run got "Unit dawn-build.scope was already
# loaded" until someone ran reset-failed by hand, with it the unit goes to
# LoadState=not-found and the next run is clean.
#
# The unit-name interlock needs a retry loop, not a one-shot failure: scope
# teardown is ASYNC, so back-to-back runs can briefly collide with a unit that
# has already exited. Retry distinguishes collision from payload failure via a
# sentinel file, because "systemd-run refused" and "your command exited 1" are
# both rc=1 otherwise -- and silently reporting a run that never happened as a
# result is exactly the class of bug this script exists to prevent.
#
# -------------------------------------------------------------------- trace ---
# A run killed by the OOM killer leaves no EXIT trap, so the record of WHAT it
# was running has to be written before it dies, by something that outlives it:
#   - `--description` puts the command in systemd's own log line, which pid 1
#     writes to /var/log/syslog: "Started dawn-build.scope - dcap: <cmd>".
#     Root-owned, rotated, survives SIGKILL and reboot.
#   - $DCAP_LOG gets an append-only line before launch, with cwd and full argv.
# The old script left neither: it passed the scope a `/tmp/dcap.XXXXXX.sh` path
# as its description, and /tmp is wiped at boot -- i.e. the name expired at
# exactly the reboot that a crash causes. On 2026-08-04 syslog recorded
# "Started run-r791b8....scope - /tmp/dcap.cfr1hb.sh" and that file was gone by
# the time anyone read the line.
#
# Usage:  dcap ./bin/dawn build ...          # default 6G
#         DCAP_MEM=8G dcap ./scripts/native-fixpoint.sh
#         DCAP_SLOTS=1 dcap ...                      # old behaviour, one at a time
# Knobs:  DCAP_MEM per-run cap (6G)   DCAP_TOTAL_MEM slice cap (14G)
#         DCAP_SWAP per-run swap (2G) DCAP_TOTAL_SWAP slice swap (4G)
# Setup:  scripts/dawn.slice must be installed; dcap tells you how if it isn't.
set -euo pipefail

MEM="${DCAP_MEM:-6G}"
SLICE="${DCAP_SLICE:-dawn.slice}"
SLOTS="${DCAP_SLOTS:-2}"
UNIT_BASE="${DCAP_UNIT:-dawn-build}"; UNIT_BASE="${UNIT_BASE%.scope}"
TOTAL_MEM="${DCAP_TOTAL_MEM:-14G}"
SWAP="${DCAP_SWAP:-2G}"
TOTAL_SWAP="${DCAP_TOTAL_SWAP:-4G}"
STATE="${DCAP_STATE:-${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$HOME/.cache}/dawn}"
LOCK="${DCAP_LOCK:-$STATE/build.lock}"   # slot i locks "$LOCK.i"
LOG="${DCAP_LOG:-$STATE/dcap.log}"
WAIT="${DCAP_WAIT:-1800}"
: "${JAVA_HOME:=$HOME/tools/graalvm-community-openjdk-21.0.2+13.1}"

case "$SLOTS" in ''|*[!0-9]*|0) echo "dcap: DCAP_SLOTS=$SLOTS is not a positive integer" >&2; exit 2 ;; esac
[ $# -gt 0 ] || { echo "dcap: nothing to run" >&2; exit 2; }
mkdir -p "$STATE"
# Payloads deliberately kept from signal-killed runs (see the end of this file)
# are the only files that outlive a run. Prune them, or this becomes the same
# litter pile the old script left in /tmp -- 213 scripts, which is itself an
# invitation to `rm /tmp/dawn*` and thereby to the lock-deletion bug above.
find "$STATE" -maxdepth 1 -name 'run.*.sh' -mtime +7 -delete 2>/dev/null || true

# --- the slice must exist and must be capped -------------------------------
# A missing slice is a silent downgrade to "no total budget", which looks
# exactly like a working guard. Fail instead.
to_bytes() {
  case "$1" in
    infinity|max) echo infinity; return 0 ;;
    *[!0-9KkMmGgTt]* | '') return 1 ;;
  esac
  local n="${1%[KkMmGgTt]}" u="${1#"${1%[KkMmGgTt]}"}"
  case "$u" in
    '')  echo "$n" ;;
    K|k) echo $(( n * 1024 )) ;;
    M|m) echo $(( n * 1024 * 1024 )) ;;
    G|g) echo $(( n * 1024 * 1024 * 1024 )) ;;
    T|t) echo $(( n * 1024 * 1024 * 1024 * 1024 )) ;;
  esac
}
mem_bytes=$(to_bytes "$MEM") || { echo "dcap: DCAP_MEM=$MEM is not a size" >&2; exit 2; }
if [ "$(systemctl show "$SLICE" -p LoadState --value 2>/dev/null || true)" != loaded ]; then
  cat >&2 <<EOF
dcap: $SLICE is not installed, so nothing would bound the TOTAL memory of
      concurrent runs -- the guard this script advertises would be absent while
      still appearing to work. Install it:

        sudo install -m 644 $(dirname "$0")/dawn.slice /etc/systemd/system/dawn.slice
        sudo systemctl daemon-reload
EOF
  exit 78
fi
# Assert the budget on the real (system) slice. Only touch it when it differs,
# so the common path costs one `systemctl show` and no sudo.
total_bytes=$(to_bytes "$TOTAL_MEM") || { echo "dcap: DCAP_TOTAL_MEM=$TOTAL_MEM is not a size" >&2; exit 2; }
to_bytes "$SWAP" >/dev/null || { echo "dcap: DCAP_SWAP=$SWAP is not a size" >&2; exit 2; }
total_swap_bytes=$(to_bytes "$TOTAL_SWAP") || { echo "dcap: DCAP_TOTAL_SWAP=$TOTAL_SWAP is not a size" >&2; exit 2; }
slice_max=$(systemctl show "$SLICE" -p MemoryMax --value 2>/dev/null || echo infinity)
slice_swap=$(systemctl show "$SLICE" -p MemorySwapMax --value 2>/dev/null || echo infinity)
if [ "$slice_max" != "$total_bytes" ] || [ "$slice_swap" != "$total_swap_bytes" ]; then
  sudo -n systemctl set-property --runtime "$SLICE" \
    MemoryMax="$TOTAL_MEM" MemorySwapMax="$TOTAL_SWAP" 2>/dev/null || true
  slice_max=$(systemctl show "$SLICE" -p MemoryMax --value 2>/dev/null || echo infinity)
fi
if [ "$slice_max" = infinity ]; then
  echo "dcap: $SLICE has MemoryMax=infinity -- the total budget is not capped." >&2
  echo "      (Query the SYSTEM manager, not --user.) Fix /etc/systemd/system/dawn.slice," >&2
  echo "      then: sudo systemctl daemon-reload" >&2
  exit 78
fi
if [ "$mem_bytes" != infinity ] && [ "$mem_bytes" -gt "$slice_max" ]; then
  cat >&2 <<EOF
dcap: DCAP_MEM=$MEM exceeds $SLICE's MemoryMax ($slice_max bytes), so the run
      would be capped lower than you asked for and die early for a reason that
      is not in its own log. Raise the total budget deliberately:

        DCAP_TOTAL_MEM=$MEM dcap ...

      (This box's VM ceiling is set in ~/.wslconfig; a build budget near it
      trades a contained cgroup kill for an uncontained host-side one.)
EOF
  exit 78
fi

# --- the payload script ----------------------------------------------------
# The command must reach the scope as a script file: this box's PATH contains
# Windows entries with parentheses, so it cannot be passed as a shell one-liner.
# It lives under $STATE, not /tmp, so a tmp cleaner cannot delete it mid-run.
RUN=$(mktemp "$STATE/run.XXXXXX.sh")
STARTED="$RUN.started"
trap 'rm -f "$RUN" "$STARTED"' EXIT
# Forward the caller's whole exported environment. Without this, `FOO=1 dcap
# ./gate.sh` silently loses FOO -- the gate runs with a different config than
# you asked for and reports success. (Learned 2026-08-04: DAWNC_BIN=... was
# dropped, the gate rebuilt its own subject, and looked green.) dcap must be
# transparent to everything except the cgroup.
{ printf '#!/usr/bin/env bash\nset -euo pipefail\n'
  printf ': > %q\n' "$STARTED"
  export -p | grep -Ev '^declare -x (SHELLOPTS|BASHOPTS|UID|EUID|PPID|BASH_VERSINFO)=' || true
  printf 'cd %q\n' "$PWD"
  printf 'exec'; printf ' %q' "$@"; printf '\n'
} > "$RUN"
chmod +x "$RUN"

# --- trace, written before anything can kill us ----------------------------
cmd_str=$(printf '%s ' "$@"); cmd_str=${cmd_str% }
desc="dcap: $cmd_str"
[ "${#desc}" -le 200 ] || desc="${desc:0:197}..."
if [ -f "$LOG" ] && [ "$(stat -c %s "$LOG" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)" -gt 2000000 ]; then
  mv -f "$LOG" "$LOG.1"
fi
printf '%s pid=%s unit=%s-N.scope slots=%s slice=%s mem=%s cwd=%s run=%s cmd=%s\n' \
  "$(date -Is)" "$$" "$UNIT_BASE" "$SLOTS" "$SLICE" "$MEM" "$PWD" "$RUN" "$cmd_str" >> "$LOG"

# --- interlocks, per slot ---------------------------------------------------
# Interlock 1 is the slot's file lock, held on fd 9 by THIS process for its
# whole life (dcap must therefore not exec itself away, which also keeps the
# EXIT trap alive). Interlock 2 is the slot's fixed unit name, checked by pid 1.
# Try slot 0, then 1, ...; the first whose lock is free AND whose unit starts
# wins. If a lock is free but the unit name is taken (async teardown, or a
# holder that bypassed the lock), release the lock and move on to the next slot.
uid=$(id -u); gid=$(id -g)
start=$(date +%s)
deadline=$(( start + WAIT ))
rc=0; UNIT=""; slot=-1; announced=0
while :; do
  for (( i = 0; i < SLOTS; i++ )); do
    exec 9>"$LOCK.$i"
    flock -n 9 || continue
    u="$UNIT_BASE-$i.scope"
    # Clear a unit left failed by a previous run before trying: keeps the
    # "Ctrl-C a build, run it again" path silent and immediate.
    if [ "$(systemctl show "$u" -p ActiveState --value 2>/dev/null || true)" = failed ]; then
      sudo -n systemctl reset-failed "$u" 2>/dev/null || true
    fi
    printf '%s pid=%s slot=%s unit=%s\n' "$(date -Is)" "$$" "$i" "$u" >> "$LOG"
    rc=0
    sudo -n systemd-run --scope --quiet --collect \
      --unit="$u" --slice="$SLICE" --description="$desc" \
      -p MemoryMax="$MEM" -p MemorySwapMax="$SWAP" \
      --uid="$uid" --gid="$gid" \
      -E HOME="$HOME" -E JAVA_HOME="$JAVA_HOME" -E PATH="$PATH" \
      "$RUN" || rc=$?
    # Sentinel present => the payload really ran, so rc is the payload's.
    # Absent => the scope never started ("systemd-run refused" and "your command
    # exited 1" are both rc=1, hence the sentinel).
    if [ -e "$STARTED" ]; then UNIT="$u"; slot=$i; break 2; fi
    # ActiveState=failed with no processes left: --collect cannot help when
    # systemd-run ITSELF was killed (Ctrl-C, harness timeout) before it could
    # deregister the scope. Unambiguous (a scope with live processes is active
    # or abandoned, never failed), so clear it.
    if [ "$(systemctl show "$u" -p ActiveState --value 2>/dev/null || true)" = failed ]; then
      sudo -n systemctl reset-failed "$u" 2>/dev/null || true
    fi
    exec 9>&-
  done
  if [ "$(date +%s)" -ge "$deadline" ]; then
    echo "dcap: all $SLOTS slot(s) still busy after ${WAIT}s -- inspect: systemctl status '$UNIT_BASE-*.scope'" >&2
    printf '%s pid=%s exit=blocked waited=%ss\n' "$(date -Is)" "$$" "$WAIT" >> "$LOG"
    exit 75
  fi
  if [ "$announced" = 0 ]; then
    echo "dcap: all $SLOTS slot(s) busy, waiting (up to ${WAIT}s)..." >&2
    announced=1
  fi
  sleep 1
done

printf '%s pid=%s slot=%s exit=%s elapsed=%ss\n' "$(date -Is)" "$$" "$slot" "$rc" "$(( $(date +%s) - start ))" >> "$LOG"

# --- interlock 2, the other half: the payload is done, so the unit must go ---
# A scope stays ACTIVE while its cgroup is non-empty, and it is not systemd-run
# that keeps it there -- systemd-run exits with the payload and systemd marks
# the scope abandoned, still holding the name. So one process that outlived the
# payload takes $UNIT hostage for as long as it runs, and because the name IS
# the mutex, every later dcap on this box blocks on it. Observed 2026-08-05: a
# gate that SIGKILLed a test driver left the grandchild spinning, and the next
# dcap spent 317 retries and its whole WAIT budget on "Failed to start
# transient scope unit" for a run that had finished hours earlier.
#
# That leak is fixed at its source (scripts/native-cli-diff.sh reaps the
# process group now), but "no gate ever leaks a child again" is not a property
# this script gets to assume, and the cost of assuming it wrongly is every
# build on the box. So: the run is over, therefore the scope is over. Anything
# still in that cgroup is by definition not the payload, and stopping the unit
# kills it.
#
# The grace loop is there because scope teardown is async -- a perfectly clean
# run can still read active for a moment, and reporting that as a leak would
# make this warning noise instead of signal.
for _ in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do
  [ "$(systemctl show "$UNIT" -p ActiveState --value 2>/dev/null || true)" = active ] || break
  sleep 0.5
done
if [ "$(systemctl show "$UNIT" -p ActiveState --value 2>/dev/null || true)" = active ]; then
  leaked=$(systemctl show "$UNIT" -p TasksCurrent --value 2>/dev/null || echo '?')
  echo "dcap: $UNIT still holds $leaked task(s) after the payload exited -- something" >&2
  echo "      leaked a child. Stopping the scope; report it rather than just retrying." >&2
  printf '%s pid=%s leaked_tasks=%s cmd=%s\n' "$(date -Is)" "$$" "$leaked" "$cmd_str" >> "$LOG"
  sudo -n systemctl stop "$UNIT" 2>/dev/null || true
  sudo -n systemctl reset-failed "$UNIT" 2>/dev/null || true
fi

# Keep the payload script when the run was killed by a signal: at that point it
# is the only copy of what was about to run, and it costs one file.
if [ "$rc" -ge 128 ]; then
  trap - EXIT; rm -f "$STARTED"
  echo "dcap: killed by signal $(( rc - 128 )); payload kept at $RUN" >&2
fi
exit "$rc"
